Continuing my hashtag#30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on one of the most important concepts for writing reliable and production-ready Ansible playbooks — Idempotency.

🎯 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗜𝗱𝗲𝗺𝗽𝗼𝘁𝗲𝗻𝗰𝘆?

In simple terms:

👉 Run the same playbook once or multiple times — the final system state should remain the same.

Ansible checks the current state before making changes.

For example:

- name: Install Nginx
 ansible.builtin.apt:
 name: nginx
 state: present

First run: ✅ Nginx gets installed
🔄 Task shows changed

Second run: ✅ Nginx is already installed
⏭️ No unnecessary change

This is what makes Ansible safe to run repeatedly.

🔍 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗻𝗴𝗲 𝗠𝗮𝗻𝗮𝗴𝗲𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁

Ansible reports whether a task:

🟢 ok → No change required
🟡 changed → Something was modified
🔴 failed → Task failed
⏭️ skipped → Condition wasn't met

You can preview what Ansible would change using:

ansible-playbook site.yml --check

And see the differences with:

ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff

💡 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗜𝗱𝗲𝗺𝗽𝗼𝘁𝗲𝗻𝗰𝘆 𝗠𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀

✔️ Safer automation
✔️ Repeatable deployments
✔️ Reduced configuration drift
✔️ Predictable infrastructure
✔️ Easier CI/CD automation
✔️ Fewer unnecessary changes

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗻𝗼𝘁 𝗷𝘂𝘀𝘁 𝗮𝗯𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝗿𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗰𝗼𝗺𝗺𝗮𝗻𝗱𝘀.

It's about declaring the desired state and allowing Ansible to make only the changes required to reach that state.

That's the mindset that makes automation production-ready. 🚀
